---
import Lede from './Lede.astro';
/**
 * PREMIER LEADERSHIP — slice R4.
 *
 * CONTEXT.md Section Composition table, row "Premier Leadership":
 *
 *   Entrance      C — IMAGE-FIRST. The photograph opens the section,
 *                 before any type, in document order AND in reading
 *                 order. Educational Leader directly above is
 *                 deliberately photo-free and opens on an outdented
 *                 kicker capped by a rule; this opens on a plate and
 *                 has no kicker and no rule at all. That contrast is
 *                 the turn between the two spreads.
 *   Column window photo 1-4, type 5-11 (band 1)
 *   Image         `column` tier — portrait-seated.jpg
 *   Rhythm        80px. Educational Leader above is 110, Everyday
 *                 Legends below is 70. Sections MUST NOT share a
 *                 padding token.
 *   Margin holds  the four speaking topics.
 *   Length        ABRIDGED — opening statement + one body paragraph +
 *                 topics + button. It has its own site.
 *
 * THE ABRIDGEMENT IS THE SPEC, NOT AN OVERSIGHT. COPY.md's note on
 * length is explicit: the word budget runs ~20 to ~110 words across
 * the page, a roughly 5:1 spread the composition depends on being
 * VISIBLE AS SECTION HEIGHT. Nothing here is lengthened to fill space
 * and nothing is restored from Appendix A1. If the section reads
 * thin, that is a composition problem and is solved in the layout.
 *
 * NAMING. The display name is "Premier Leadership" — no LLC suffix
 * anywhere in this section. COPY.md puts the legal form in exactly
 * one place on the whole site, the About paragraph, where the client
 * wrote it herself; it is not propagated here.
 *
 * NO EMAIL ADDRESS APPEARS IN THIS FILE, in markup or in a comment.
 * The address carried in the source copy is superseded per COPY.md
 * and MUST NOT be published; the Premier Leadership address that did
 * replace it belongs to the contact form (Phase F), not to this
 * section. The WEBSITE is the section's only outbound destination and
 * it is carried by the button.
 *
 * NO HEIGHT CONSTRAINT. No min-height, no svh floor. CONTEXT.md's
 * R1.4 standing rule: the Cover is the only section on this page with
 * one. This section is content-height, and R4.2 inverted which content
 * sets it: the plate used to be the tallest thing in the section and
 * now it is the shortest of the three columns, taking its height from
 * the type beside it.
 *
 * NO grid-column IS SET IN THIS FILE. Four collapse-aware primitives
 * carry every placement — .win-prem-plate, -head, -body, -margin —
 * and each one's behaviour in all four bands (>=1280 / 1024-1279 /
 * 751-1023 / <=750) is written out in full above the implementation
 * in global.css, before the implementation.
 *
 * ONE grid-row IS SET, on .win-prem-plate, and it is STRUCTURAL
 * rather than a measurement: the type column is authored in exactly
 * four blocks, so the plate spans from the first to the last. It is
 * not a line count, it does not go stale when the copy rewraps, and
 * it is released to `auto` the moment the section stacks at 1121.
 * The TOPICS are seated by document order alone — authored
 * immediately after the body — with no offset and no line count.
 *
 * R4.2 — THE PLATE'S BOTTOM EDGE IS THE BUTTON'S BOTTOM EDGE, and it
 * is solved structurally rather than with a number. The plate no
 * longer carries `align-self: start` or an aspect ratio in the
 * side-by-side band; it stretches across its four-row span, and the
 * track list on `.prem` below makes the fourth of those rows exactly
 * one button tall by giving the topics list a fifth, FLEXIBLE row to
 * spill into. Grid sizes items spanning a flexible track in a step
 * that distributes space only to flexible tracks, so the topics can
 * no longer inflate the button's row — which, measured, they were
 * doing at every width in both 12-column bands.
 *
 * R4.2 — THE SCRIM IS DELETED, with no replacement. See the plate
 * comment in the markup.
 *
 * NO TRANSFORM ON THE PHOTOGRAPH, EVER. The crop is done by
 * oversizing the <img> box inside overflow:hidden. A static
 * transform: scale() on the <img> silently kills the
 * mix-blend-mode treatment — measured in R1, the treated backdrop
 * reverted to the raw source colour — because the transformed image
 * composites into its own layer.
 *
 * GOLD: ZERO NEW. Both budgets are spent. The plate carries the
 * Cover's already-shipped flat Gold multiply at 0.24 from
 * .plate-img::before, which is reuse of the page's one photograph
 * treatment — the budget counts type accents and hairline rule
 * contexts, and this is neither. No rule exists in this section, so
 * it adds 0 of the 8 rule contexts, and no type is gold.
 *
 * OVERLAP: ZERO. Two of the three page overlaps are spent (the Cover
 * name, the Athletic detail crop) and the third is reserved for R6.
 * Nothing in this section overlaps anything.
 */
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import portraitSeated from '../assets/portrait-seated.jpg';

/* THE TOPICS PANEL'S SUBMARK. Inlined the way EverydayLegends.astro
 * inlines the Foundation mark: `?raw` from brand_assets/, prepared at
 * inline time, THE FILE ON DISK NEVER EDITED.
 *   1. <metadata> (the C2PA manifest) and the c2pa namespace go.
 *   2. role="img" goes: the mark is aria-hidden at the placement, so a
 *      role would announce nothing.
 *   3. THE THREE FAINT PATHS GO — #FDFDFD at 7.1% and 2.7%, #C4C4C3 at
 *      4.7%. Rendered over Cashmere, the mark with and without them is
 *      indistinguishable; flattened to one ink they would print as
 *      solid shapes. Same finding as the Legends mark.
 *   4. The root keeps fill="currentColor", so the ink is the
 *      placement's `color`: Obsidian. */
import submarkRaw from '../../brand_assets/mcclain-submark.svg?raw';
const submarkSvg = submarkRaw
  .replace(/<metadata>[\s\S]*?<\/metadata>/, '')
  .replace(/\s+xmlns:c2pa="[^"]*"/, '')
  .replace(/\s+role="img"/, '')
  .replace(/<path\b[^>]*\bfill="#(FDFDFD|C4C4C3)"[^>]*?\/?>/gi, '');
---

<section class="sheet prem" id="sec-pillar">
  <!--
    SOURCE ORDER IS STRUCTURAL. plate -> head -> lede -> body ->
    button -> topics. The first five are main's arrangement, unchanged.
    The topics band comes LAST, so grid's sparse cursor puts it in a
    row of its own after the button at every width; see the band's
    comment below and global.css. Reordering these siblings changes
    the composition.

    It is also the reading order at every width: photograph, name,
    subtitle, opening statement, body, button, then the four topics.
    The button is a grid item of its own rather than a child of the
    body, so it keeps its own row.
  -->
  <figure class="win-prem-plate prem-plate plate-draw" style="--curtain-exit: -1">
    <!--
      THE PLATE. `column` tier — half the page or less, inside the
      body window. It CANNOT bleed: the bleed-left tier is spent on
      Athletic Management, which is the page's single spread moment
      and the only place the page gutter is broken.

      Two things stop it reading as pasted on here, and R4.2 removed
      a third that never worked:

      (a) THE CROP. Her figure crosses the plate's bottom edge and
          both side edges — at the shoulder line and below, the
          blazer is cut by the left and right trims, so the backdrop
          never terminates against a vertical edge inside the frame.
          Measured off the source, not eyeballed; the numbers are on
          .prem-frame below.
      (b) THREE SHARED EDGES. The plate's top edge sits on the
          section's top padding line, its left edge on the sheet's
          left trim, and — R4.2 — its bottom edge on the button's
          bottom edge. A rectangle sharing three edges with the page's
          own structure reads as an aperture; one sharing none reads
          as a card.

      THE SCRIM IS GONE AND MUST NOT COME BACK IN ANY FORM. R4.1
      shipped a page-ground gradient across the top of this plate to
      dissolve its top edge. The mechanism cannot work on this frame:
      her hair enters at 11.41% of the plate's height, so any scrim
      that clears her hair is confined to the top 11%, and 11% of a
      frame cannot dissolve its edge. What shipped was one soft edge
      against three hard ones, which reads as a light leak rather than
      as a device. It is also no longer needed — at 421.3px wide the
      plate reads as a portrait plate rather than as a card, which is
      R4.1's own finding. No gradient, no mask, no feathered edge
      replaces it. CONTEXT.md carries the general rule: measure where
      the subject enters the frame before specifying a scrim at all.

      THE PLATE CARRIES THE COVER'S FLAT GOLD AT 0.24 FROM
      .plate-img::before, AND NOTHING ELSE.

      No border, no border-radius, no shadow — CONTEXT.md's standing
      rule for every photograph on the page.

      Eager + sync decode: a photograph that resolves late is absent
      from full-page captures and from any PDF the client generates.
    -->
    <div class="plate-img prem-frame">
      <!--
        `sizes` DESCRIBES THE <img> BOX, WHICH IS 161.6% OF THE PLATE.
        The box is oversized on purpose — that is how the crop is done
        without a transform — so the browser must be told the box's
        width, not the plate's, or it picks a rendition sized for the
        visible window and upsamples it by 1.6x.

        Recomputed for the 1-4 plate. Measured, per band:
          <=750    plate is the full sheet   box 1083px at 750  = 144vw
          <=1023   plate is 6 of 8           box 1135px at 1023 = 111vw
          <=1121   plate is 9 of 12, stacked box 1194px at 1121 = 107vw
          else     plate is 4 of 12          box  681px at 1440 =  47vw
        48vw carries the side-by-side band with a little headroom. The
        previous 60vw was computed against the 1-5 plate and was left
        over-declared by the narrowing in this pass — safe, but it
        fetches a larger file than the layout needs.
      -->
      <Image
        src={portraitSeated}
        alt="Dr. Syreeta McClain seated in a leopard-print blazer against a warm tan studio backdrop."
        widths={[640, 960, 1280, 1720]}
        sizes="(max-width: 750px) 144vw, (max-width: 1023px) 111vw, (max-width: 1121px) 107vw, 48vw"
        loading="eager"
        decoding="sync"
      />
        <!--
      R18.2 — THE PLATE FRAME DRAW. A CURTAIN, AND IT IS A SIBLING OF
      THE PHOTOGRAPH, NEVER A WRAPPER ROUND IT.

      R1.1 is the whole reason this element exists in this shape. A
      transform on the img promotes it to its own compositing layer
      and .plate-img::before's mix-blend-mode: multiply silently stops
      blending — measured then at R-B falling from 8 to -1 and
      luminance returning to the raw source value, and CONTEXT.md
      records that the transformed element itself breaks it too, not
      only an ancestor. So the thing that moves is neither the img nor
      any ancestor of it: it is this div, painted OVER the finished
      composite at z-index 3. The multiply is re-measured on every
      plate after this ships rather than reasoned about.

      It paints the page's own ground via --page-ground with
      background-attachment: fixed — R16's mechanism, for R16's
      reason: a fixed-attachment background is positioned against the
      VIEWPORT, so this box paints the pixels the body would have
      painted behind it. aria-hidden and pointer-events: none: it is
      a transient covering, not content.
    -->
    <div class="plate-curtain" aria-hidden="true"></div>
    </div>
  </figure>

  <div class="win-prem-head prem-head">
    <!--
      THE <h2> IS THE SECTION NAME. CONTEXT.md's locked heading
      structure: each section's <h2> is the element whose text names
      the section, and that string equals the folio label. Verified
      against src/data/sections.ts — { id: 'sec-pillar', n: '04',
      label: 'Premier Leadership' } — not assumed. No LLC suffix.

      NO OUTDENT AND NO RULE ABOVE IT. Educational Leader owns that
      entrance and repeating it here would make two adjacent sections
      open the same way, which CONTEXT.md names as the failure mode.
      The head sits at the top of the type block, on the type block's
      own left edge.
    -->
    <h2 class="role prem-name">Premier Leadership</h2>
    <p class="prem-subtitle">Founder &amp; CEO</p>
  </div>

  <!-- R18.1 — THE LEDE LINE MASK. The island nests INSIDE this
  paragraph; it does not replace it. The paragraph is the grid item
  and carries this section's placement, and Astro wraps a hydrated
  island in a real astro-island element — so an island mounted in
  the paragraph's place would become the grid item itself and move a
  left edge, which is the one thing this round may not do. Classes,
  placement and box are untouched. Only the text node moves into the
  island, and the island renders that same string verbatim until it
  has measured the rendered line boxes. Copy is COPY.md's. -->
  <p class="win-prem-head prem-lede"><Lede text="Leadership is not a position. It is the responsibility to move people, ideas, and organizations forward." /></p>

  <div class="win-prem-body prem-body">
    <p>Twenty years of leading schools, building programs, and navigating complex systems. Premier&nbsp;Leadership brings that experience beyond the schoolhouse to help others turn vision into action.</p>
  </div>


  <!--
    Website only. The button IS the link — COPY.md gives this section
    one destination and one button, and printing the bare domain as
    well would be the redundancy that was removed from Everyday
    Legends for the same reason. rel="noopener", not
    "noopener noreferrer": noreferrer strips the Referer header and
    costs Anchor referral attribution from every client site it ships.
  -->
  <p class="win-prem-body prem-cta">
    <a class="btn-solid" href="https://www.premierleadersllc.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Explore Premier&nbsp;Leadership<span class="vh"> (opens in new tab)</span></a>
  </p>

  <!--
    THE TOPICS BAND — the four speaking and workshop topics, in a
    full-width Cashmere band below the photograph and the text, at
    every width. AUTHORED LAST, after the button: spanning 1 / -1, it
    is auto-placed into the first row the plate leaves free, which is
    the row after the button. Authored before the button, it would
    push the button below itself. It is also the reading order:
    photograph, name, subtitle, opening statement, body, button, then
    the topics.

    A <ul>, because four topics are a list. NO bullets, no numerals,
    no rules between items — a numeral here would be countable and
    would collide with the Index's own 01-05 numbering.
  -->
  <div class="win-prem-margin prem-margin">
    <span class="prem-mark" aria-hidden="true" set:html={submarkSvg}></span>
    <p class="prem-margin-label">Speaking topics</p>
    <ul class="prem-topics">
      <li>Alternative&nbsp;&amp; <span class="keep">Non-Traditional Education</span></li>
      <li>Leadership Development</li>
      <li>Parenting Athletes</li>
      <li>NIL Management</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  /* CONTEXT.md's per-section padding table gives Premier Leadership
     80. Educational Leader above is 110 and Everyday Legends below is
     70; sections MUST NOT share a padding token, so the value is
     declared here and nowhere else.

     FLAT AT EVERY BAND, which is R3's scaling pattern read off the
     build rather than invented: EducationalLeader.astro declares
     `padding-block: 110px` once with no band override anywhere in the
     file, so its 110 is the same 110 at 1440 and at 375. This section
     follows it exactly — one declaration, no media query. Inventing a
     scale here would put R4 on a rule R3 does not use.

     THE ROW TRACK LIST. FIVE rows, and only the fifth is flexible.
     Rows 1-4 are the type column's four authored blocks — head, lede,
     body, button. Row 5 holds nothing of its own.

     R4 shipped four rows with the LAST one 1fr, because the plate was
     then the tallest thing in the section and its surplus had to land
     somewhere other than the internal type gaps, which are derived
     from the type tokens and must not be added to.

     R4.2 INVERTS THAT. The plate is no longer taller than the type —
     it stretches to the type — so the surplus it used to shed no
     longer exists. What does exist, and what R4.1 did not measure, is
     the TOPICS LIST: spanning the body's row and the button's row, it
     was taller than both together at every width in both 12-column
     bands, and it was setting the button's row height. Row 4 rendered
     82.86px at 1440 against a 48.5px button, 137.42px at 1279 and
     173.80px at 1122. Nothing looked wrong, because a grid row grows
     downward and the button never moved — but the row's BOTTOM edge
     was the list's, not the button's, and R4.2 needs that edge.

     SO ROW 5 IS THE SPILL ROW AND IT IS THE ONLY FLEXIBLE TRACK.
     CSS Grid sizes items that span a flexible track in a separate
     step from items that do not, and that step distributes space ONLY
     to the flexible tracks in the span. The topics list spans rows
     3-5, so it contributes NOTHING to the body's row or the button's
     row and all of its surplus lands in row 5, below the button,
     where it is ordinary section space — the same place R4 was
     putting the plate's surplus, for a different reason.

     Row 5 collapses to zero at any width where the list is shorter
     than the body and the button together. Nothing here is a
     measurement and nothing goes stale when the copy rewraps.

     THE TOPICS BAND ROUND: the list no longer spills into row 5. It
     IS row 5, the full-width band below the plate and the button.
     The track list is main's, unchanged, so rows 1-4 and the plate
     spanning them are main's too. */
  .prem{
    padding-block: 80px;
    grid-template-rows: auto auto auto auto 1fr;
  }

  /* ---- THE PLATE — entrance C ------------------------------------ */

  .prem-plate{ margin: 0; }

  /* 4:5. CONTEXT.md: no two photographs on the page share a crop, and
     aspect ratio is a separate lever from width. The Cover's plate is
     a full-height column-tall bleed; this one is a fixed 4:5
     rectangle.

     THE CROP, measured off the source rather than eyeballed.
     portrait-seated.jpg is 4267x6198, EXIF orientation 1. Her figure
     was swept row by row against the backdrop to find, at each source
     row, the leftmost and rightmost pixel that is NOT backdrop:

         source row    subject spans
             2299      1249 - 3498     hair and the near shoulder
             2499       809 - 3538     THE SHOULDER LINE, her widest
             3199       710 - 3677
             3799       620 - 3767
             4299       560 - 3757     widest point of the sleeves
             4699       779 - 3388     hands and lap

     The visible window is [840, 3480] x [150, 3450] of the source.
     Its left and right trims sit INSIDE her shoulder span at every
     row from 2499 down, so she is cut by both side edges rather than
     sitting inside the rectangle; and its bottom trim at 3450 is
     mid-blazer, so she crosses that too. Backdrop survives only above
     the shoulder line, beside her head — an organic termination, not
     a straight edge inside the frame.

     THE MECHANISM IS AN OVERSIZED BOX, NOT A TRANSFORM. Measured in
     R1: a static transform: scale() on the <img> takes the treated
     backdrop from #a5a29d back to the raw #b3b4b4, because the
     transformed image composites into its own layer and
     .plate-img::before can no longer blend against it. The reasoning
     that a transform is safe "because it is on a sibling of ::before,
     not an ancestor" is wrong in practice.

     So: a width greater than 100% with a matching negative left, both
     percentages, clipped by .plate-img's overflow: hidden.

         --crop-w   4267 / 2640            = 161.6%
         --crop-l   -(840 x 1.6163 / 4267) = -31.82%
         --crop-y    150 / 2864.5          = 5.18%

     Both horizontal terms are percentages of the plate's own width,
     so plate width cancels out of the source window entirely and the
     crop is IDENTICAL at every viewport width in every band — no
     drift, no breakpoint, nothing to re-measure. The box aspect is
     1.29 against the file's native 0.688, comfortably wider, so
     object-fit spreads the full source width across the box at every
     width and the identity holds. */
  /* R4.2 — THE HEIGHT COMES FROM THE GRID, NOT FROM AN ASPECT RATIO.
     `height: 100%` against the stretched figure, which is stretched
     across rows 1-4, whose bottom is the button's bottom. The 4:5 that
     used to live here is restored in the <=1121 block, where the
     section stacks and the plate is alone in its row — there,
     stretching it to its row would be stretching it to itself, so it
     needs an intrinsic height again.

     THE CROP IS UNAFFECTED HORIZONTALLY. Both horizontal terms are
     percentages of the plate's own width, so the source window's left
     and right trims are identical before and after this change. The
     box aspect at 1440 goes from 1.29 to 1.25, still comfortably wider
     than the file's native 0.688, so object-fit still spreads the full
     source width across the box and the identity holds. What changes
     is vertical: a taller box at the same width means she crosses the
     bottom trim harder, which is the intended consequence. */
  .prem-frame{
    height: 100%;
    --crop-w: 161.6%;
    --crop-l: -31.82%;
    --crop-y: 5.18%;
  }

  /* :global() — <Image> emits its own <img>, which never receives
     Astro's data-astro-cid-*, so a scoped selector silently stops
     matching. This has bitten this project twice.

     max-width: none is not optional. Tailwind's preflight sets
     `img { max-width: 100% }`, which clamps the oversized box back to
     the plate width and produces no crop at all. */
  .prem-frame :global(img){
    position: absolute;
    top: 0;
    left: var(--crop-l);
    width: var(--crop-w);
    max-width: none;
    height: 100%;
    object-fit: cover;
    object-position: 50% var(--crop-y);
  }

  /* ---- THE HEAD -------------------------------------------------
     No kicker outdent and no rule. The head sits at the top of the
     type block on the type block's own left edge, in the established
     .role register — 500 weight, uppercase, 0.14em, Obsidian — which
     is a HEAVIER and DARKER register than Educational Leader's 10.5px
     Slate kicker directly above it. Two adjacent sections must not
     announce themselves the same way. */
  .prem-head{
    /* HEAD -> LEDE. One body line, via the body's own tokens rather
       than a pixel: calc(1.0 x 17px x 1.8) = 30.6px. It re-resolves
       from the type at every width with no breakpoint. A fixed pixel
       gap set from one width is a swept band by another name. */
    margin-bottom: calc(1.0 * var(--bio-size) * var(--bio-lh));
  }

  .prem-name{
    margin: 0;
    margin-left: -0.07em;   /* half of .role's 0.14em tracking */
  }

  /* The deck. SENTENCE CASE, deliberately: the name above it is
     uppercase and letterspaced, so setting the subtitle the same way
     would give the head two identical-looking lines and no hierarchy.
     Case is the differentiator, not size alone. Same reasoning and
     same register as Educational Leader's subtitle, which is the
     point — the SUBTITLE is a shared device across the pillars; the
     entrance is not. Set verbatim from COPY.md. */
  .prem-subtitle{
    margin: 2px 0 0;
    font-family: var(--sans);
    font-weight: 300;
    font-size: 12px;
    line-height: 1.5;
    letter-spacing: 0.012em;
    color: var(--slate);
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }

  /* ---- LEDE ------------------------------------------------------
     Tier 1 of 3, on the shared --lede-size token so R5's decorative
     mark can derive its own size from the same number. Same face,
     same four axes, same line-height and letterfit as Educational
     Leader's and About's ledes — CONTEXT.md's lede rule. The section
     differs from its neighbours in ENTRANCE, WINDOW and RHYTHM, which
     is where the difference belongs; re-scaling the lede would break
     the page's one display tier into two that mean nothing.

     3.22x the 17px body at 1440, inside CONTEXT.md's 3-4x band.

     NO max-width. Measure is controlled by the column span and by
     nothing else. */
  .prem-lede{
    /* LEDE -> BODY. 0.75 of the lede's OWN line-height via `lh`, so
       it re-resolves from the type rather than from a width. R3
       precedent, same number, same unit. */
    margin: 0 0 0.75lh;
    font-family: var(--display);
    font-variation-settings: var(--fx-lede);
    font-weight: 350;
    font-size: var(--lede-size);
    line-height: var(--lede-lh);
    letter-spacing: -0.018em;
    color: var(--obsidian);
    text-wrap: balance;
  }

  /* ---- BODY ------------------------------------------------------
     Tier 3 of 3. --bio-size / --bio-lh, the page's body tokens,
     unchanged. NO max-width — span only. One paragraph, per COPY.md.
     Nothing is restored from Appendix A1. */
  .prem-body{
    font-family: var(--sans);
    font-weight: 300;
    font-size: var(--bio-size);
    line-height: var(--bio-lh);
    letter-spacing: 0.004em;
    color: var(--slate);
    text-wrap: pretty;
    orphans: 3;
    widows: 3;
    /* BODY -> BUTTON. calc(1.75 x 17px x 1.8) = 53.55px, again from
       the body's own tokens. Larger than the head->lede gap and
       larger than the lede->body gap, so the button reads as leaving
       the text block rather than as its fourth paragraph. */
    margin-bottom: calc(1.75 * var(--bio-size) * var(--bio-lh));
  }

  .prem-body p{ margin: 0; }

  /* The margin-bottom above belongs to the paragraph block; the CTA
     shares the same primitive and must not repeat it.

     R4.2 — `display: flex` SO THE PARAGRAPH BOX IS EXACTLY THE BUTTON.
     The button is an inline-block, so as a line box the paragraph
     carried the strut's descent below it: 57.8px of paragraph against
     48.5px of button at 1440. That difference is invisible on its own
     — nothing is drawn in it — but the plate's bottom edge is now this
     row's bottom edge, so a paragraph taller than its button would put
     the plate ~9px below the button and reproduce the near-miss R4.2
     exists to remove. One flex item, no strut, so the box is the
     button's height exactly. The button's TOP does not move: it was
     already the line box's top, the inline-block being taller than the
     strut's ascent. Nothing about the button's own appearance changes
     — it keeps its intrinsic width, its left alignment and every
     interactive state. */
  .prem-cta{ margin: 0; display: flex; }

  /* ---- THE TOPICS BAND — the four speaking topics ---------------
     A full-width Cashmere band in its own row below the photograph and
     the text, at every width. Square, no border, no shadow.

     SPAN. 1 / -1 (global.css): from the plate's left edge, which is
     column 1, to the right content edge, which is where the
     masthead's CONTENTS link ends. It never runs into the gutter.

     THE PLATE DOES NOT SEE IT. The plate spans rows 1-4 (head, lede,
     body, button) and the band sits in row 5, so the plate's height
     and its bottom edge on the button are main's exactly.

     GAP ABOVE: --s5. Two heavy horizontal edges meet here, the plate's
     and the button's foot above and the band's top below, and a gap
     that reads as a step rather than a near miss needs the page-level
     token, not the paragraph one (R4.2's lesson). Section padding is
     main's 80, unchanged.

     PADDING: --s4 top and bottom, --s2 either side. Everything inside
     is centred, so the side padding only binds on phones, and --s2 is
     what it has to be there: at 360 with a 15px scrollbar the band is
     297px, and the widest phrase that must hold a line at 19.5px,
     "Non-Traditional Education", is 262.14px. 297 - 32 = 265 holds it;
     --s3 left 249px and it overflowed from 360 to 372, and "Leadership
     Development" (252.33px) orphaned from 360 to 363. */
  .prem-margin{
    margin-top: var(--s5);
    padding: var(--s4) var(--s2);
    background: var(--cashmere);
    text-align: center;
    font-size: 10.5px;
    line-height: calc(var(--bio-size) * var(--bio-lh));
  }

  /* THE SUBMARK, SIZED AGAINST THE LABEL. The label sets on the
     body's line, 30.6px, and the mark is TWO of the label's line
     boxes tall (61.2px). The width follows from the mark's own viewBox
     (1597.74 x 1618.49), never set. Obsidian, so it is not a gold use.
     The gap under it is half a label line. Centred by the mark's box
     (its viewBox), not by its ink. */
  .prem-mark{
    display: block;
    height: 2lh;
    margin-bottom: 0.5lh;
    color: var(--obsidian);
  }

  .prem-mark :global(svg){
    display: block;
    height: 100%;
    width: auto;
    margin-inline: auto;
  }

  /* Same size, weight, tracking, case and colour as
     .legends-margin-label, except:

     FULL-STRENGTH SLATE, NOT 0.82. The 0.82 knock-down was sized for
     Porcelain (4.90:1). On Cashmere it composites to about 4.0:1,
     under AA for 10.5px type.

     CENTRED. Letter-spacing adds 0.22em after the last letter too, so
     a centred line would sit 0.11em left of centre. Widening the box
     by that 0.22em on the right puts the letters on the centre line. */
  .prem-margin-label{
    margin: 0;
    margin-right: -0.22em;          /* the trailing 0.22em of tracking */
    text-align: center;
    font-family: var(--sans);
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: 10.5px;
    letter-spacing: 0.22em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--slate);
  }

  /* THE TOPICS. 1.5 x the 13px they were set at in the margin, so
     19.5px, written as that ratio. Smoked Slate at full strength. A
     stacked list, one topic to a line, each line centred.

     ONE BLOCK, NOT FOUR ITEMS. The old margin list opened a 1.4lh gap
     between items; here the items sit on the list's own leading with
     half a line between them, so the four read as one block.

     THE PHONE WRAP (Jackson's ruling). At 19.5px the longest topic is
     394.69px, and a 360 phone has 312px of content. Below about 508px
     (524 with a 15px scrollbar) it cannot hold one line, and it sets
     on two, balanced and centred. The markup binds it so the only
     break is after the ampersand: "Alternative &" / "Non-Traditional
     Education". The half-line item gap is what keeps those two lines
     reading as one item: it is wider than the leading inside the
     wrapped item. --s2 under the label, so the list is clear of the
     tracked caps without drifting away from them.

     NO bullets, no numerals, no rules between items, no gold. */
  .prem-topics{
    margin: var(--s2) 0 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
    font-family: var(--sans);
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: calc(13px * 1.5);
    line-height: 1.4;
    letter-spacing: 0.01em;
    color: var(--slate);
    text-wrap: balance;
  }

  .prem-topics li + li{ margin-top: 0.5lh; }


  /* ---- BAND 2's STACKING CROSSOVER · <= 1121 --------------------
     THE SECTION STACKS EARLY. See the measured 1121 and the rule that
     produced it in global.css; the primitives there move every window
     and what is left here is the one thing a span cannot express.

     The plate takes a block gap under it rather than sitting beside
     type. Two body lines — from the body's own tokens, not a pixel —
     the same value band 3 uses, because it is the same relationship
     in the same stacked arrangement.

     The row track list comes off: there are no longer four rows for
     the plate to span. The topics band follows the button here too,
     in its own row, with the same --s5 above it. */
  @media (max-width: 1121px){
    .prem{ grid-template-rows: none; }
    .prem-plate{ margin-bottom: calc(2.0 * var(--bio-size) * var(--bio-lh)); }

    /* R4.2 — AN INTRINSIC ASPECT COMES BACK, and it has to. Stacked,
       the plate is alone in its row, so `height: 100%` resolves
       against a row whose height is the plate's own — a circular
       definition that renders as zero. The aspect ratio is the plate's
       intrinsic height in every stacked band. The bottom-edge
       alignment does not exist here and cannot: the photograph is
       ABOVE the type, not beside it, so there are no two horizontal
       edges near each other at the foot of the section for it to fix.

       R6.1 — 0.82, NOT 4:5. The stacked plate was 4:5 = exactly 0.800
       from R4 through R6, and so is the Cover's plate at every width
       from 800 down: the Cover's `min(125vw, max(100vw, 118svh))`
       resolves to 1/1.25 on a full-width plate, and 1/1.25 is 0.800.
       Two photographs on one page sharing an EXACT crop is the one
       thing CONTEXT.md's rule forbids outright.

       THE COVER'S EXPRESSION IS NOT THE ONE TO MOVE. It is R1
       territory, it carries a portrait-safety floor and an svh cap
       that hold the frame square on short landscape viewports, and it
       is load-bearing in a way a plain ratio is not. This one is a
       single number.

       0.82 CLEARS BOTH NEIGHBOURS. It sits 0.02 from the Cover's 0.800
       and 0.12 from the Athletic detail's 0.700. The rule being
       enforced is exact equality — the page already ships pairs 0.013
       apart at other widths and those are accepted — so 0.02 is not a
       near-miss to be widened, it is a clearance.

       THE CROP SURVIVES IT, and that was checked from the render
       rather than assumed. Both horizontal crop terms are percentages
       of the plate's OWN width, so the source window's left and right
       trims do not move at all and she is still cut by both side
       trims. Only the vertical changes: the img box aspect goes from
       1.616 x 0.80 = 1.293 to 1.616 x 0.82 = 1.325, still far wider
       than the file's native 0.688, so object-fit still spreads the
       full source width and the visible vertical fraction narrows from
       0.532 to 0.519. With --crop-y unchanged at 5.18% the window top
       moves from 0.0242 to 0.0249 of the source — seven ten-thousandths,
       nowhere near the crown of her head — and the bottom trim rises
       from 0.5566 to 0.5442, so she crosses it slightly harder, which
       is the direction R4.2 already moved it deliberately. */
    .prem-frame{ aspect-ratio: 0.82; height: auto; }
  }

  /* ---- BAND 3 · 751-1023 -----------------------------------------
     Already stacked by the block above; band 3 only narrows the sheet
     to 8 tracks and moves the windows. Nothing here needs restating —
     the rules above hold through both bands, which is the point of
     keeping this file's cascade one-directional. */
  @media (max-width: 1023px){

    /* The plate runs 6 of 8 columns here — 702.3px at 1023, against
       666px at 1024 in the sub-band above, so the boundary is nearly
       seamless. It keeps its 4:5 crop unchanged; the crop is
       expressed in percentages of the plate's own width and is
       identical at every width in every band. */
  }

</style>
